 "url", "label": "url", "type": "STRING", "localized_name": "url"}, {"name": "response", "label": "response", "type": "STRING", "localized_name": "response"}], "color": "#432", "widgets_values": ["Create a 16:9 widescreen cinematic film pre-production board / storyboard for a \"Generic High-Performance Vehicle Ad\".\n\nMULTI-REFERENCE ADAPTATION RULES:\n- VEHICLE / MAIN SUBJECT: Strictly adapt from REFERENCE IMAGE 1 (the first uploaded image). This can be any vehicle (sports car, truck, hovercraft, anime-style vehicle, fantasy wagon, mech-car, etc.). Extract its design language, color palette, material, and unique visual traits.\n- ENVIRONMENT / LOCATION: \n  - If REFERENCE IMAGE 2 (second uploaded image) is provided → MUST use that image's environment as the racing location. Analyze its architecture, lighting, terrain, atmosphere.\n  - If NO second image provided → default to using the background/environment from REFERENCE IMAGE 1 as the location.\n\nLayout structure (strictly follow this grid):\n- Dark premium background with thin white or light-colored structural dividing lines.\n- Top header (Art Direction zone): Display text \"16:9 Performance Vehicle Short Film\", \"8 Key Shots\", \"Vehicle Style (inferred from Ref1)\", \"Location (inferred from Ref2 or Ref1's bg)\", \"Color Palette (extract 5 colors from Ref1)\", \"Keywords (infer 5 mood keywords)\".\n- Left column (Vehicle & Driver Reference): Show the vehicle's front/side/key detail close-ups (headlight/wheel/hover unit/unique feature). Inside cockpit: a driver (always inside, face NOT visible, clothing style adapted from Ref1's aesthetic). NO standing outside.\n- Mid-Upper area (Environment Design): Show environment according to the rule above. Add a top-down path diagram with a colored line marking the vehicle's route, labeled with: \"Camera Position\", \"Tracking Shot\", \"Turn Zone\", \"Low Angle\", \"In-Cockpit\", \"Aerial/Drone\".\n- Middle section (8-panel storyboard, 2 rows x 4 columns, each cell 16:9 aspect ratio). Show these 8 shots in order, adapting ALL visual logic to match Ref1 (vehicle) and Ref2/location (environment):\n  1. Wide establishing shot of environment (from Ref2 or Ref1 bg), vehicle enters.\n  2. Low-angle push forward, front detail.\n  3. In-cockpit view, driver from behind or side angle (face hidden, adapted clothing).\n  4. Macro close-up of propulsion system (wheel/track/hover unit/energy vent/mech leg).\n  5. High-speed cornering/drifting/turning, with motion blur or energy trails.\n  6. Rear tracking shot, light/energy trails.\n  7. High-angle / drone / aerial overhead shot, vehicle moving along path.\n  8. Hero final shot: vehicle parked or paused under dramatic lighting, reflecting the world.\n  Below each cell: small info bar with \"Shot Type / Scale / Movement / Action / Emotion\".\n- Bottom bar (Lighting & Mood): Infer from both references. Keywords, audio atmosphere, shot language.\n\nStrict rules:\n- Driver ALWAYS inside vehicle. Face NOT visible.\n- Driver clothing style adapts to Ref1's aesthetic.\n- NO standing outside the vehicle.\n- If Ref1 shows non-wheeled vehicle (hover/flying/legged), replace shot #4 and #5 accordingly.\n- If Ref1 is anime/comic style → cel shading, bold lines, anime lighting.\n- If Ref1 is abstract/surreal → non-real physics and dreamlike motion.\n- If Ref2 is provided, environment MUST match Ref2 exactly. If not, use Ref1's background.\n\nOutput: Professional, clean, premium commercial ad storyboard, cinema-grade质感, grid clearly visible, visually coherent with both references.\n--ar 16:9", "2k", "high", "3:4", false, 2121970590, "randomize"], "inputs": [{"name": "image1", "link": 121, "label": "image1", "type": "IMAGE", "localized_name": "image1"}, {"shape": 7, "name": "image2", "link": 118, "label": "image2", "type": "IMAGE", "localized_name": "image2"}, {"shape": 7, "name": "image3", "label": "image3", "type": "IMAGE", "localized_name": "image3"}, {"shape": 7, "name": "image4", "label": "image4", "ty